{"id":"f14t2","name":"Weekly Merchant Payouts","lang":"pyspark","entry":"solve","params":["spark","transactions_df","until"],"limit_s":30,"ordered":true,"desc":"<p>Our marketplace pays its merchants once a week. <code>transactions_df</code> has the columns <code>txn_id</code>, <code>merchant</code>, <code>created_at</code> (timestamp), <code>kind</code> (<code>sale</code>, <code>refund</code> or <code>fee</code>, in any case) and <code>amount</code> (double, always positive). The payment provider sometimes sends the same transaction twice; a <code>txn_id</code> is one transaction.</p>\n<p>A payout week starts on a Monday and covers Monday to Sunday. The net of a week is its sales minus its refunds minus its fees. To protect itself against future refunds, the marketplace keeps a <b>reserve</b> of 10% of the sales of each week and gives it back to the merchant exactly 4 weeks later, with the payout of that later week. So the payout of a week is its net, minus 10% of its sales, plus 10% of the sales of the week 4 weeks before. This is true even for a week in which the merchant sold nothing: a quiet week can still have a payout because a reserve is released.</p>\n<p>The report is produced on the date <code>until</code> (string <code>yyyy-MM-dd</code>); transactions after that day are not known yet.</p>\n<p>Your task is to return, for each merchant, one row for every week from the week of its first transaction to the week that contains <code>until</code>, with the columns <code>merchant</code>, <code>week</code> (date of the Monday), <code>sales</code> (double, total sales of the week) and <code>payout</code> (double), both rounded to 2 decimals, sorted by <code>merchant</code>, then <code>week</code>.</p>\n<h4>Example</h4><p><code>transactions_df</code>:</p><div class=\"tblw\"><table><tr><th>txn_id</th><th>merchant</th><th>created_at</th><th>kind</th><th>amount</th></tr><tr><td>t1</td><td>m1</td><td>2026-03-02 10:00:00</td><td>sale</td><td>100.0</td></tr><tr><td>t2</td><td>m1</td><td>2026-03-08 23:30:00</td><td>Sale</td><td>50.0</td></tr><tr><td>t2</td><td>m1</td><td>2026-03-08 23:30:00</td><td>Sale</td><td>50.0</td></tr><tr><td>t3</td><td>m1</td><td>2026-03-04 12:00:00</td><td>FEE</td><td>3.0</td></tr><tr><td>t4</td><td>m1</td><td>2026-03-17 09:00:00</td><td>refund</td><td>20.0</td></tr><tr><td>t5</td><td>m2</td><td>2026-03-23 09:00:00</td><td>sale</td><td>80.0</td></tr><tr><td>t6</td><td>m2</td><td>2026-04-15 09:00:00</td><td>sale</td><td>999.0</td></tr><tr><td>t7</td><td>m2</td><td>2026-04-10 09:00:00</td><td>sale</td><td>500.0</td></tr></table></div><p><code>until = \"2026-04-08\"</code></p><p>the output should be</p><div class=\"tblw\"><table><tr><th>merchant</th><th>week</th><th>sales</th><th>payout</th></tr><tr><td>m1</td><td>2026-03-02</td><td>150.0</td><td>132.0</td></tr><tr><td>m1</td><td>2026-03-09</td><td>0.0</td><td>0.0</td></tr><tr><td>m1</td><td>2026-03-16</td><td>0.0</td><td>-20.0</td></tr><tr><td>m1</td><td>2026-03-23</td><td>0.0</td><td>0.0</td></tr><tr><td>m1</td><td>2026-03-30</td><td>0.0</td><td>15.0</td></tr><tr><td>m1</td><td>2026-04-06</td><td>0.0</td><td>0.0</td></tr><tr><td>m2</td><td>2026-03-23</td><td>80.0</td><td>72.0</td></tr><tr><td>m2</td><td>2026-03-30</td><td>0.0</td><td>0.0</td></tr><tr><td>m2</td><td>2026-04-06</td><td>0.0</td><td>0.0</td></tr></table></div><div class=\"lim\"><b>[execution time limit]</b> 30 seconds (pyspark)<br><b>[memory limit]</b> 2 GB<br><b>[input]</b> DataFrame transactions_df<br><b>[output]</b> DataFrame with exactly the columns <code>merchant</code>, <code>week</code>, <code>sales</code>, <code>payout</code>, in this order; rows in the order given by the statement</div>","starter":"from pyspark.sql import SparkSession, DataFrame, functions as F\nfrom pyspark.sql.window import Window\n\n\ndef solve(spark: SparkSession, transactions_df: DataFrame, until: str) -> DataFrame:\n    # write your code here\n    return transactions_df\n","hints":[],"summary_fr":"","tags":["consigne longue","semaines sans activité","réserve libérée 4 semaines plus tard","grille de dates"],"minutes":22,"source":"Kata Lab","howto":"<h5>Où écrire</h5><ol><li>Écris dans l'éditeur à droite, fichier <code>solution.py</code>.</li><li>Complète la fonction <code>solve(spark, transactions_df, until)</code>. Garde son nom et ses paramètres : ce sont les tests qui l'appellent, avec leurs propres données. N'écris pas d'appel à la fonction toi-même.</li><li><code>spark</code> est une SparkSession déjà prête : ne crée pas la tienne.</li><li><code>transactions_df</code> est un DataFrame déjà chargé : ses colonnes sont celles de l'énoncé (onglet <b>Input</b> d'un test pour les voir).</li></ol><h5>Ce que tu dois renvoyer</h5><ol><li>Ta fonction doit <b>renvoyer</b> le résultat avec <code>return</code>. Un <code>print</code> ou un <code>show()</code> ne compte pas : il sort seulement dans l'onglet <b>Console Output</b>.</li><li>Le DataFrame renvoyé doit avoir <b>exactement 4 colonnes</b>, avec ces noms et dans cet ordre : <code>merchant</code>, <code>week</code>, <code>sales</code>, <code>payout</code>. Une colonne en plus (une colonne de calcul oubliée), un nom différent ou un autre ordre et le test est faux, même si les valeurs sont justes. Finis par un <code>select(...)</code> de ces colonnes.</li><li><b>L'ordre des lignes compte</b> : trie exactement comme l'énoncé le demande (<code>orderBy</code> à la fin).</li><li>Valeurs : les nombres sont comparés à 0,000001 près : arrondis seulement là où l'énoncé le demande, et au nombre de décimales demandé ; les dates s'affichent AAAA-MM-JJ.</li></ol><h5>Lancer et vérifier</h5><ol><li>Spark tourne dans ton Codespace : au premier RUN TESTS de la séance, attends qu'il soit prêt (1 à 2 minutes). Ensuite chaque lancement prend quelques secondes.</li><li>Clique <b>RUN TESTS</b> (ou <code>Ctrl + Entrée</code>) : tes 2 tests visibles tournent.</li><li>Quand c'est bon, tu vois en vert <b>« Tests passed: 2/2. »</b>. Sinon, ouvre le test marqué <b>Wrong answer</b> : compare « Expected return value » et « Your return value », et clique <b>VIEW DIFF</b> pour colorer les cases différentes. <b>Runtime error</b> veut dire que ton code a planté : le message est dans le test.</li><li>Clique <b>SUBMIT</b> : ta note sur 100 compte aussi 6 tests cachés. Soumets dès que quelques tests passent, puis améliore et resoumets : c'est la dernière soumission qui compte.</li></ol>","files":null,"visible":[{"input":{"transactions_df":{"__df__":{"schema":"txn_id string, merchant string, created_at timestamp, kind string, amount double","rows":[["t1","m1","2026-03-02 10:00:00","sale",100.0],["t2","m1","2026-03-08 23:30:00","Sale",50.0],["t2","m1","2026-03-08 23:30:00","Sale",50.0],["t3","m1","2026-03-04 12:00:00","FEE",3.0],["t4","m1","2026-03-17 09:00:00","refund",20.0],["t5","m2","2026-03-23 09:00:00","sale",80.0],["t6","m2","2026-04-15 09:00:00","sale",999.0],["t7","m2","2026-04-10 09:00:00","sale",500.0]]}},"until":"2026-04-08"},"expected":{"columns":["merchant","week","sales","payout"],"rows":[["m1","2026-03-02",150.0,132.0],["m1","2026-03-09",0.0,0.0],["m1","2026-03-16",0.0,-20.0],["m1","2026-03-23",0.0,0.0],["m1","2026-03-30",0.0,15.0],["m1","2026-04-06",0.0,0.0],["m2","2026-03-23",80.0,72.0],["m2","2026-03-30",0.0,0.0],["m2","2026-04-06",0.0,0.0]]}},{"input":{"transactions_df":{"__df__":{"schema":"txn_id string, merchant string, created_at timestamp, kind string, amount double","rows":[["a","x","2026-01-05 08:00:00","sale",10.0]]}},"until":"2026-02-02"},"expected":{"columns":["merchant","week","sales","payout"],"rows":[["x","2026-01-05",10.0,9.0],["x","2026-01-12",0.0,0.0],["x","2026-01-19",0.0,0.0],["x","2026-01-26",0.0,0.0],["x","2026-02-02",0.0,1.0]]}}],"hidden_n":6,"hidden":"","solution":""}